defklown wrote:
> Let's say my strength is 18/03. This means that my strength is 18 and
> is roughly 3% of the way to 19, unless I'm mistaken.

Str counts up to 18 and then continues with 18/01 to 18/99 and finally
18/100 which is displayed as 18/**. Above that value strength is counted
as 19, 20, up to 25 and requires rings of gain strength or/and gauntlets
of power to get these values.

> How would a strength of 19 be displayed? 18/100?

A diplayed "19" actually is "18/** + 1".
18/100 is displayed as 18/**.

> How would a strength of 22 be displayed? 18/400?

Displayed as "22" (which is 18/** + 4).
A strength of "four points more than 18" is 18/04.

> What on earth does a strength of 18/** mean? ** is a not a number!

18/** is 18/100. (I suppose it's to just save a digit.)

> There is water, holy water, and unholy water. So does that mean
> cursed/uncursed/blessed status does not apply to water?

Cursed water has an own name, "unholy water".
Blessed water has an own name, "holy water".

> As in, you
> can't have cursed holy water, uncursed holy water, or blessed holy
> water - you just have holy water (though going through the blessing
> process for normal water turns it into holy water). What if one dips an
> unidentified, cursed potion into a potion of water, twice. What will
> the cursed potion turn into?

On first dip a cursed item becomes uncursed, on second dip blessed.

If you dip a stack of ordinary (uncursed) water into a potion of holy
water you bless the whole stack. (Use unholy water to unbless/curse
objects.)

> If I have a gem and I deduce that it is a rare and valuable gem, how do
> I properly identify it as one, so that the shopkeeper will actually pay
> the proper sum for it?

You need to identify the gem by magic means, either (and most common)
by a scroll of identify, or by the magic spell of same name. Sometimes
there are events in Nethack that will also identify objects in your
inventory.

